=> MVC (Model-View-Controller) is an architectural pattern that separates an application into three components: Model, View, and Controller. The Model manages data and business logic, the View displays the user interface, and the Controller handles user requests, interacts with the Model, and returns the appropriate View.

=> The View interacts with the Controller by sending HTTP requests (GET or POST). These requests are triggered by user actions such as clicking a link, submitting a form, or making an AJAX request
Ex: 
Form Submission: 
<form asp-action="Save" method="post">
    <input type="text" name="Name" />
    <button type="submit">Save</button>
</form>

Button click:
<a asp-controller="Home" asp-action="About">About</a>

=> Attribute Routing is a feature in ASP.NET MVC that allows developers to define URL patterns directly on controllers or action methods using attributes like [Route]. It provides more flexibility and makes routing easier to understand and maintain because the route definition is located alongside the action it applies to.

=> Bundling in ASP.NET MVC is a technique used to combine multiple CSS and JavaScript files into a single file. This reduces the number of HTTP requests made by the browser, which improves the application's loading performance. It is commonly used with minification, which reduces the size of the bundled files by removing unnecessary characters.

Why is Bundling Needed?
Imagine your application has these files:

bootstrap.css, site.css, style.css

jquery.js, bootstrap.js, validation.js

Without bundling, the browser makes 6 separate HTTP requests.

With bundling:

styles.bundle.css
scripts.bundle.js

Now the browser makes only 2 HTTP requests, which improves page load time.

=> Minification is the process of removing unnecessary characters (spaces, comments, line breaks) to reduce file size.

Bundling
1. Combines multiple files into one
2. Reduces HTTP requests
3. Improves loading performance

Minification
1. Reduces the file size by removing whitespace, comments, and unnecessary characters
2. Reduces download size
3. Improves download speed

=> A partial class is a C# feature that allows a class to be split across multiple source files using the partial keyword. During compilation, all the parts are combined into a single class. This is useful when multiple developers work on the same class or when part of the code is auto-generated.
Ex: 
Employee.cs:
public partial class Employee
{
    public int Id { get; set; }

    public void Display()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Employee Details");
    }
}

EmployeeDetails.cs:
public partial class Employee
{
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public void ShowName()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Name);
    }
}

Rules for Partial Classes
1. Every part must use the partial keyword.
2. All parts must have the same class name.
3. All parts must be in the same namespace.
4. At compile time, they become one class.


=> ASP.NET Web API is a framework for building HTTP-based RESTful services. It allows different applications, such as web applications, mobile apps, and desktop applications, to communicate with a server using HTTP methods like GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE. Web APIs typically return data in JSON format instead of HTML.


=> REST stands for Representational State Transfer. It is an architectural style used to design web services that communicate over HTTP. A REST-based service is called a RESTful service, and the API that follows REST principles is called a REST API.
A REST API is a web service that follows REST principles and uses HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) to perform operations on resources.

A RESTful service should:
Use HTTP methods correctly (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)
Identify resources using URLs
Be stateless (the server does not remember previous requests)
Return standard HTTP status codes (such as 200 OK, 201 Created, 404 Not Found)
Usually exchange data in JSON format


=> WCF (Windows Communication Foundation) is a Microsoft framework for building distributed applications that communicate using multiple protocols such as HTTP, TCP, Named Pipes, and MSMQ. It supports both SOAP and REST services. ASP.NET Web API is a lightweight framework designed primarily for building RESTful HTTP services that exchange data in JSON format. Web API is the preferred choice for modern web, mobile, and cloud applications, while WCF is mainly used in legacy enterprise systems or when SOAP and multiple transport protocols are required.

WCF	ASP.NET Web API
Older Microsoft technology	Modern framework for HTTP APIs
Can use HTTP, TCP, Named Pipes, MSMQ, etc.	Uses HTTP/HTTPS
Can build SOAP services	Primarily builds RESTful APIs
XML is the default format	JSON is the default format
More configuration	Simpler and lightweight
Better for legacy enterprise systems	Better for web, mobile, and SPA applications
Uses [ServiceContract] and [OperationContract]	Uses controllers and HTTP attributes like [HttpGet]

=> An ASP.NET MVC application can call a Web API using HttpClient. The MVC controller sends HTTP requests (GET, POST, PUT, or DELETE) to the Web API, receives a JSON response, deserializes it into C# objects, and passes those objects to the view for display.

=> Filters in ASP.NET MVC are attributes that execute code before or after an action method or action result. They are used for cross-cutting concerns such as authentication, authorization, logging, caching, and exception handling, helping to keep controller actions clean and reusable.

Types of filters:
Authorization Filter
Action Filter
Result Filter
Exception Filter
Authentication Filter (or authentication middleware in ASP.NET Core)

=> A Session is used to store user-specific data on the server across multiple requests.
HTTP is stateless, which means each request is independent. Session helps remember information about a user while they are browsing your application.

Session is a server-side state management technique used to store user-specific data across multiple HTTP requests. Since HTTP is stateless, sessions help maintain information such as the logged-in user, shopping cart, or user preferences. In ASP.NET MVC, session values can be stored, retrieved, updated, and removed using the Session object (MVC 5) or HttpContext.Session (ASP.NET Core).

=> SOAP stands for Simple Object Access Protocol. SOAP messages are always sent in XML format. A SOAP service is a web service that follows the Simple Object Access Protocol. It exchanges messages in XML format and follows a strict protocol with standards for security, reliability, and transactions. SOAP services are commonly used in legacy enterprise applications.

=> Lazy Loading is a technique where related data is loaded only when it is actually needed, instead of loading it immediately.
Ex:
public class Employee
{
    public int Id { get; set; }

    public string Name { get; set; }

    public virtual Department Department { get; set; }
}
The virtual keyword allows Entity Framework (EF6) to create a proxy object that supports lazy loading.
For eager loading we use include()
Employee emp = db.Employees.Include(e => e.Department).First();

=>IEnumerable<T> is an interface that is used to iterate over a collection already loaded into memory.
IQueryable<T> is an interface that allows LINQ queries to be translated into SQL and executed on the database server.

IEnumerable	IQueryable
Works with in-memory collections	Works with database queries
Filtering happens in application memory	Filtering happens in the database
Uses LINQ to Objects	Uses LINQ to Entities (or another LINQ provider)
Suitable for small collections	Better for large datasets
Executes after data is loaded into memory	Translates LINQ into SQL before execution
Less efficient for large database queries	More efficient for database queries


=> Encapsulation is the process of wrapping everything in a single entity(class). 
Here class contains variables and methods.
But variables are exposed to outside we will make it private and stop access.
We can allow them to access through methods inside.
Ex:
class BankAccount {

    private int balance = 1000;   // Hidden

    public void withdraw(int amount) {
	if(amount <= balance){
              balance = balance - amount;
	}
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
